WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Practical Application of Defect Opportunities

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.266
Welcome to this session on defect opportunities.

3
00:00:08.266 --> 00:00:13.232
We’re going to study that by looking at the ranch here, the Golden Spur Ranch.

4
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:17.433
And look at a performance audit and management system

5
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:23.033
that has been based on defects per million opportunities.

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:30.033
Now, you'll recall that we looked at a simple layout. In this case, we’re looking at buildings,

7
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:35.599
rooms specifically, all the rooms that are used here

8
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.600
at the ranch, in various buildings.

9
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.233
And we have things like air conditioners, appliances,

10
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.599
fixtures, lighting, and so on. Roof, walls.

11
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.766
Those are the column variables, here where the cursor’s at and these are the rooms.

12
00:00:50.766 --> 00:00:56.666
And we have some notation: clean, adjust, fix, repair, and stock.

13
00:00:56.666 --> 00:00:59.666
So, these are the opportunities.

14
00:00:59.666 --> 00:01:04.732
Now basically, little X’s goes in these boxes to show applicability.

15
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:10.799
Then during audit, for those where there are X’s they’re looked at and rated.

16
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.100
Clean, adjust, fix, replace, stock and so on.

17
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.300
That's done for vehicles, tools, animals, feed, tack,

18
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:27.266
fences, grounds, a lot of stuff. Pools.

19
00:01:27.266 --> 00:01:34.232
Now, that basically boils down to being able to create several different kinds of metrics:

20
00:01:34.233 --> 00:01:38.233
quality, safety, productivity, attendance, and savings.

21
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:42.633
Now with quality, we simply count discrepancies.

22
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:46.033
See, this is what we count for the metric.

23
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:51.599
Quality is discrepancies. Safety is incidents.

24
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.033
Productivity is carryovers. Now, what do we mean by that?

25
00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:59.299
Those are things that didn't get fixed that were supposed to have gotten fixed.

26
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.300
They’re carryovers. And that's a productivity issue.

27
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:09.033
Attendance we use lost hours, hours missed.

28
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:13.699
And of course savings, the count is actions.

29
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.900
Now, for the base, the base is opportunity.

30
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.900
That's all those little cells in the matrix, we just looked at.

31
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.433
Every one of those is an opportunity.

32
00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:31.599
Safety is actual hours. Hours is the opportunity base.

33
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.666
Productivity is discrepancies. How many discrepancies were carried over and not fixed.

34
00:02:37.666 --> 00:02:41.666
Attendance is expected hours. That's the base.

35
00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:45.899
And savings of course is opportunities. Savings opportunities.

36
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.800
Now, from that we weight them. In this case. Quality has a 25%.

37
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.500
Safety and productivity are the big hitters.

38
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.466
And attendance and savings is less weighted.

39
00:02:57.466 --> 00:03:03.199
In turn, that helps us to form a bonus pool for the employees at the ranch.

40
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.466
Now the calculations are pretty simple.

41
00:03:06.466 --> 00:03:11.566
Step 1 is A over B. So, we take the count over the base.

42
00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:17.699
That's equal to C. We multiply C by a million. From there we see a table.

43
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.633
Now, if we take a look at the various categories.

44
00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:27.633
In this case, buildings had 88 rows and 21 columns,

45
00:03:27.633 --> 00:03:33.366
thereby forming 1,848 opportunities.

46
00:03:33.366 --> 00:03:36.732
If I do that for each of the categories,

47
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:42.333
we discover a total of 6,288 opportunities

48
00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:49.166
for something to go astray with respect to value here at the ranch.

49
00:03:49.166 --> 00:03:52.732
Now, by keeping track of that.

50
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:58.366
For example, in March 1, 2003 goal was Five Sigma.

51
00:03:58.366 --> 00:04:05.166
The baseline in March of 2001 was running about, in the audits,

52
00:04:05.166 --> 00:04:12.366
about 101, or discrepancies per audit, or about 3.6 Sigma.

53
00:04:12.366 --> 00:04:16.632
So, the goal for one year is to get that up to four,

54
00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:22.599
and the goal, ultimately, in March 2003 was to get that to Five Sigma.

55
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:28.200
So, we wanted to make about a half a Sigma improvement in the course of one year.

56
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.900
And if you notice, we benchmark around here,

57
00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.900
we do the ranch work about as good as airlines handle bags.

58
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:41.666
The key is we recognize that, and we're setting goals to become world-class

59
00:04:41.666 --> 00:04:45.666
in how we operate and run the ranch.

60
00:04:45.666 --> 00:04:51.832
Now again, there's another simple chart lined out, showing those things.

61
00:04:51.833 --> 00:04:58.766
And we have the weekly reports, and our PPM conversion charts

62
00:04:58.766 --> 00:05:01.732
showing the rates of improvement.

63
00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:06.466
We started with the baseline of 3.65 Sigma, that we just saw.

64
00:05:06.466 --> 00:05:10.399
We set a weekly rate of improvement of 1.8%,

65
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.233
in order to get us to the goal of Four Sigma at the end of 12 months.

66
00:05:15.233 --> 00:05:22.266
So basically, I need to improve by about 2% weekly. That's our goal here at the ranch.

67
00:05:22.266 --> 00:05:29.266
And that gives us our quality, productivity, safety, attendance charts.

68
00:05:29.266 --> 00:05:36.299
In this case we’re looking at this part of the six up chart, showing four of the six key metrics.

69
00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:42.566
And that is how we apply the idea of defect opportunities.

70
00:05:42.566 --> 00:05:47.566
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
